  "files": [
    {
      "content": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\n\n/*\n * Each body holds coordinate positions (i.e., x, y, and z) and\n * velocities (i.e., vx, vy, and vz).\n */\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\n\n/*\n * Read a binary dataset with initilized bodies.\n */\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) {\n  int b = fread(nbodies, sizeof(*nbodies), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError reading nbodie value\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n  Body *bodies = (Body *)malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError reading input values\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n\n  return bodies;\n}\n\n/*\n * Write simulation results into a binary dataset.\n */\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\n  int b = fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError writing to output\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n}\n\nint main(int argc,char **argv) {\n  int nbodies;\n\n  Body *bodies = read_dataset(&nbodies);\n\n  /*\n   * One parallel region for the whole simulation: each iteration uses two\n   * worksharing loops with static (contiguous) scheduling. Each body's force\n   * accumulation is performed by a single thread in serial order, so results\n   * are bitwise identical to the sequential reference.\n   */\n  #pragma omp parallel\n  {\n    for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; ++iter) {\n      /* Compute interbody forces and update velocities. */\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n        float fx = 0.0f;\n        float fy = 0.0f;\n        float fz = 0.0f;\n\n        for (int j = 0; j < nbodies; ++j) {\n          float dx = bodies[j].x - bodies[i].x;\n          float dy = bodies[j].y - bodies[i].y;\n          float dz = bodies[j].z - bodies[i].z;\n          float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n          float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n          float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n\n          fx += dx * inv_dist3;\n          fy += dy * inv_dist3;\n          fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n        }\n\n        bodies[i].vx += DT*fx;\n        bodies[i].vy += DT*fy;\n        bodies[i].vz += DT*fz;\n      }\n\n      /* Update positions only after all forces have been computed. */\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n        bodies[i].x += bodies[i].vx * DT;\n        bodies[i].y += bodies[i].vy * DT;\n        bodies[i].z += bodies[i].vz * DT;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\n\n  free(bodies);\n\n  exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n}\n",
      "path": "src/nbody.c"
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "Using a single OpenMP parallel region across all simulation iterations with static contiguous scheduling for both the force and position loops reduces thread-team creation overhead while preserving deterministic per-body accumulation order, improving performance on 4 cores.",
  "notes": "The entire iteration loop is wrapped in one #pragma omp parallel region. Each body's force computation remains serial over j within a single thread, so floating-point results are bitwise identical to the sequential reference. Static contiguous chunks reduce false sharing compared with interleaved chunk-1 scheduling used in earlier experiments.",
